## THE FIXED TEXT PARTS (approved byte for byte by Tuesday's read-whole; ASCII; one line each; paths and lines at main a91753cd, blobs as pinned)
- `<LABELS-GATE>` = ai-assistant, b1069-finding, qa-gate-finding
- `<MOVE-T913>` = This ticket moves to Testing next, under the project's work-tracking rule for a code change (merged, and main's push Build for the merge commit reads success); nothing is deployed.
- `<MOVE-N>` = This ticket does not move in this record; its status is unchanged.
- `<T1-SUMMARY>` = RD-913 follow-up: no cell pins the compression level of the data export, so dropping the level option at backend/dataExport.js:216 passes every committed cell (low)
- `<T1-BLUF>` = NexusAI #152 (RD-913, merge commit a91753cd) moved the data export to archiver 8, and its new characterisation cells pin the entry names, entry bytes, method, manifest and totals of an export, but not how far an entry is compressed. backend/dataExport.js line 216 builds the archive with zlib level 9. The review dropped that option in a copy and every committed cell in its run set stayed green; only the review's own per-entry compressed-size row told the two apart. Nothing is wrong today. The gap is that a change of the level, or a library default that moves, would pass the whole suite while changing the size of a customer's export ZIP (not its contents).
- `<T1-REC>` = Add one cell to __tests__/rd913-archiver-sqlite3-characterisation.test.js that exports a fixture holding one compressible entry and asserts its compressed size is below a bound that level 9 meets and the library default misses, red first by dropping the level option. Test only, no product code; it moves scripts/verify-expected-counts.json (5287 tests in 312 suites at main a91753cd) by the cell it adds. The bound must hold on node 26 and node 24, because compressed size is the runtime's zlib: on node 24 ten entries compressed to different sizes than on node 26 for the same input. Likelihood is low and nothing is failing.
- `<T1-D1>` = Measured by the review at the reviewed head (node 26 host, node 24 on alpine arm64 and amd64): the option dropped, every cell of the characterisation file and of the related export suites stayed green; the review's own row separated it (a compressible entry: 279 bytes at level 9, 561 bytes at the default; 18 entries differ). Cell A4 asserts the method is deflate (8) and nothing asserts a size. Node 24 against node 26 for the same input and no archiver at all: audit_log.sqlite 3092 bytes against 2993, the zlib versions differing (1.3.2.1-motley against 1.2.12), 10 entries differ, decompressed bytes equal.
- `<T1-D2>` = Not known: how a customer's export size would change in practice. Related: RD-913, RD-638, RD-791. Done when: a cell asserts a compressible entry's compressed size is below a bound that dropping the level option exceeds, red first, passing on node 26 and node 24; or the product owner has chosen to leave it and this ticket is closed with the decision recorded.
- `<T2-SUMMARY>` = RD-913 follow-up: the scoped jest transform in package.json has no cell for its default entry and names its plugin instead of resolving it, so a bare jest run from outside the repository root cannot load it (low)
- `<T2-BLUF>` = NexusAI #152 (RD-913, merge commit a91753cd) added a transform key to the jest block in package.json (lines 108-120) with two entries: a scoped one that runs babel-jest with the commonjs plugin on archiver and four of its dependencies (they are ES modules), and the default entry for every other project file (line 119), which keeps babel-jest on the project's own code. Two gaps, found by the review; neither fails today. First, no committed cell needs the default entry: the review removed it and 693 of 693 cells in the related and jest.mock-using suites stayed green, although the review's own probe shows that without it a require placed above a jest.mock line is no longer hoisted. Second, the plugin is named at line 115, so jest resolves it from the working directory: run from outside the repository root, jest fails to load it ("Cannot find module" for the commonjs plugin). The repository's verify script changes into the root first, and an npm run with --prefix from outside the tree passed 19 of 19, so only a bare jest from elsewhere fails.
- `<T2-REC>` = (1) Add one cell that requires a target above its jest.mock line and asserts the mock holds, red first by removing the default entry. (2) Move the jest block to a jest.config.js where the plugin is named by require.resolve, so a run from any directory loads it. Test and config only; no product file. It moves scripts/verify-expected-counts.json by the cell it adds. Likelihood is low.
- `<T2-D1>` = Measured by the review: with a transform key present jest stops applying babel-jest to project files unless the default entry is listed; the hoist probe reads mocked under the committed configuration and real without the default entry; no cell in the 77 suites run (all 69 with jest.mock, the related set, the pins) depends on it. The scoped key takes 25 files, all in the five packages plus one CommonJS file of is-stream 2.0.1, and nothing outside them. The jest block sits at package.json lines 97-122.
- `<T2-D2>` = Not known: any suite outside the 77 that relies on the default entry. Related: RD-913, RD-134 (all 12 schedulers untested because node-cron loads an ES-only package under jest; its owner should know this transform block now exists), RD-668 (the Key Vault SDK cannot load under jest). Done when: a red-first cell holds the default entry and the plugin loads from a run outside the repository root; or the product owner has chosen to leave it and this ticket is closed with the decision recorded.
- `<T3-SUMMARY>` = RD-913 follow-up: streamExport (backend/dataExport.js) stays pending when its destination is destroyed without an error, still lists a file that vanished after the listing, and has no cell for its warning branches (low)
- `<T3-BLUF>` = Three behaviours of streamExport in backend/dataExport.js, found by the review of NexusAI #152 (RD-913, merge commit a91753cd). They are the same at the old main and the new one, so the archiver upgrade did not cause them. (a) If the destination stream is destroyed with no error, the returned promise is still pending at the review's 5 second bound, at archiver 7 and at archiver 8; a destination destroyed with an error rejects. Whether POST /api/admin/export is affected is not measured. (b) If a file is removed after the listing, archiver raises a warning with code ENOENT, line 224 returns without a word, and the ZIP lacks the file while manifest.json and the returned filesIncluded still list it, so the export overstates itself. (c) The two warning branches (lines 224 and 225) and the finalize catch (lines 379-382) have no committed cell the review could find; the review did not reach the finalize catch either, because its probe on an unreadable store left finalize neither resolved nor rejected within the bound, at both versions.
- `<T3-REC>` = First decide what (a) and (b) should do: reject on a destroyed destination and drop the vanished file from the manifest, or keep the current behaviour and say so; then one red-first cell for each of the three branches. A code change in backend/dataExport.js with its own review gate; it moves scripts/verify-expected-counts.json by the cells it adds. Likelihood is low and nothing is failing.
